{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Compiler: Concept and Use "}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you talk about compilers. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common simple incidents at work in a simple spoken or written situation with a supportive interlocutor. Represent the solution outline to the problem presented by the customer through making case of use diagrams, facing the problem to the customer following rules and protocols of the organization."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation.  Exchanges simple information on programming tools and solutions to a client's requirement."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Verbs with Infinitives in Questions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "In English, there are some verbs that are always followed by other verbs in the <span class=\"strong\"> infinitive form </span>, for example, the verb <span class=\"strong\"> want </span>. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "These verbs are always followed by the preposition <span class=\"strong\"> to </span> and they can express different meanings. When you use the infinitive verb <span class=\"strong\"> want to </span>, it refers to the desire or the aim of doing or getting something:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"> I <span class=\"strong\"> want to </span> learn how to use a compiler. <br/> Do you <span class=\"strong\"> want to </span> learn how to use a compiler?  </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "There are some other meanings when using other infinitive verbs such as: <br/> <li> Verbs that express thinking or feeling: <span class=\"italic\"> decide, choose, hope, like, prefer, expect, </span> etc.</li>  <li> Verbs of speaking: <span class=\"italic\"> promise, agree, refuse, </span> etc.</li>  <li> Other verbs: <span class=\"italic\"> want to, have, help, want, try, need, manage, plan, need, </span> etc. </li>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use verbs and modal verbs followed by <span class=\"strong\"> infinitives </span> in questions. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "For instance: <br/>\t <span class=\"italic\"> Do you <span class=\"strong\"> want to </span> transform source code from a program into another computer language? <br/> What do you <span class=\"strong\"> want to </span> use a standard or a source to code compiler? <br/> Do you <span class=\"strong\"> have to </span> look for a compiler? <br/> What do you <span class=\"strong\"> need to </span> know to run a compiler? <br/> </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look at the chart to see how to build questions using verbs with infinitives: to see how to build questions using verbs with infinitives:"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":5,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"WH WORD\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"AUXILIARY VERB\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"SUBJECT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"VERB WITH INFINITIVE\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"COMPLEMENT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Do </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">you</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">want to</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">learn how to use a compiler?</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">What </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">do </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">you</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">want to</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">use a standard or a source to code compiler?</span>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Do you want to know how it works?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Do you want to give me some examples?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Choose two types of compilers."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look for detailed information about them."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a comparative chart with the information you found."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Explain your chart to your instructor. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I choose two types of compilers."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I make a comparative chart."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain my chart to my instructor. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Diagram of Operation of a Typical Compiler"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Compilers </span> in general use to translate codes from high level programming language to a lower level.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "The operation of a typical <span class=\"strong\"> compiler </span> goes through some important <span class=\"strong\"> stages </span>: <br/><br/> <ol>  <li>The source code language passes first by a lexical, syntactical (parsing) and semantical analysis. </li>  <li> Then, the compiler generates an intermediate code or language. </li> <li> After this, the code goes to an intermediate code optimizer. </li> <li>Once the intermediate code passes the error checking and the data is validated, it ends up in the generation of the object code. </li> <br/>  Look at the diagram to identify the stages of the compilation process:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Look at this diagram! When a compiler is running, it makes a lexical analysis, a parsing and a semantic analysis of the source language statements. The compiler generates an intermediate code and it seeks to match the information correctly to build and optimize the output code."}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02img01.jpg"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "target "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "relating to a goal"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This is a program that can process data from a source language to translate it into a target language."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "executable file"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a file that contains encoded data"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: So, when you want to create an executable file, do you have to use a compiler?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "encoded "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "containing encrypted information"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: A compiler creates the encoded instructions of an executable."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "parsing "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "refers to a syntactic analysis of a computer language"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When a compiler is running, it makes a lexical analysis, a parsing and a semantic analysis of the source language statements."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "output code"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the resulting code after a compilation process "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The compiler generates an intermediate code and it seeks to match the information correctly to build and optimize the output code. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "run "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to execute an operation  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: if doesn't matter if the compiler runs in a different system from the one where the executable runs.    "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob02aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe8n2le03ob02re2vid01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Mike:</span> What do you need to do when you want to transform the source code into a computer language? <br/>"}, {"time": "5000", "image": "", "id": 1, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Tutor:</span> You need to use a compiler. This is a program that can process data from a source language to translate it into a target language. The output or result of the compilation process is an object code that can be read by a processor. <br/></div>"}, {"time": "20500", "image": "", "id": 2, "text": "<div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Mike:</span> So, when you want to create an executable file, do you have to use a compiler? <br/>"}, {"time": "26000", "image": "", "id": 3, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Tutor: </span> That's right. A compiler creates the encoded instructions of an executable. <br/>"}, {"time": "31000", "image": "", "id": 4, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Mike:</span> How does a compiler work? <br/>"}, {"time": "33000", "image": "", "id": 5, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Tutor: </span> Do you want to know how it works? \u2026 Look at this diagram! When a compiler is running, it makes a lexical analysis, a parsing and a semantic analysis of the source language statements. The compiler generates an intermediate code and it seeks to match the information correctly to build and optimize the output code. <br/>"}, {"time": "54000", "image": "", "id": 6, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Mike:</span> Are all compilers similar? <br/>"}, {"time": "56000", "image": "", "id": 7, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Tutor: </span> No, there are different kinds of compilers. Programmers use most compilers to translate a high level programming language to a lower level. However, there are some that have different features. <br/>"}, {"time": "67000", "image": "", "id": 8, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Mike:</span> For instance? Do you want to give me some examples? <br/>"}, {"time": "70000", "image": "", "id": 9, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Tutor: </span> For example, decompilers. These programs can do the opposite process, they translate from a low level to a more difficult level of computer language. We also have cross-compilers; these kinds of compilers are able to run in different types of operating systems. It doesn't matter if the compiler runs in a different system from the one where the executable runs. <br/>"}, {"time": "92500", "image": "", "id": 10, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Mike:</span> Are there source-to-source compilers? <br/>"}, {"time": "95000", "image": "", "id": 11, "text": "</div><div align=\u00a8justify\u00a8><span class=\"strong\"> Tutor: </span> Yes, they are known as transpilers too, and they can translate source code with the same high level programming language.</div>"}]}, "type": "video"}], "type": "video", "_data": [], "id": 3, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can say what a compiler is."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about different types of compilers."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list the operations compilers can perform."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What type of source code editor do you use? Why?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Why is it necessary to translate a source code into a machine language?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask you partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "What do you know about comput